<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Richard de Clare, 2nd Earl of Pembroke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1130–1176</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Strongbow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Richard de Clare, known as Strongbow, was a Cambro-Norman lord who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-norman-invasion-of-ireland">Anglo-Norman invasion of Ireland</a>. Invited by the deposed king of Leinster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a>, he landed in 1170, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and married Diarmait's daughter Aoife. On Diarmait's death in 1171 he claimed Leinster, which Henry II granted him as a lordship under the crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke.jpg" alt="Richard de Clare, 2nd Earl of Pembroke" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlexD · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CoA_Gilbert_de_Clare.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
